1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Hello. A failing request and a bad answer look identical from the outside,

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:09,000
but they live in completely different layers. One is your integration code. The other is the

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:14,000
model's output. Knowing which layer broke is the first move before you touch anything.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:20,000
This lesson maps that line. Let's dig in. Let's start with three ideas that anchor the

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:26,000
whole debugging process. First, trace analysis. A trace is the ordered record of everything that

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:31,000
happened, your prompts, the tool calls, the tool results, any thinking, and the errors.

7
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Reading it top to bottom shows you where behavior first diverged from what you intended.

8
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Second, problem origin isolation. Before you edit a single line, you localize the failure to one

9
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,000
layer, either your integration code or the model's output. And third, origin evidence. That is the

10
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:58,000
specific mid-trace tell, a wrong tool argument, an empty tool result, or a truncated response

11
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:03,000
that points you at the layer that actually broke. Nail these three and the fix gets obvious.

12
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Here's the split that organizes everything. On one side is the integration layer. That's

13
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:16,000
everything wrapping the model, your API request, the endpoint, auth, how you parse the response,

14
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:23,000
and your tool harness. When this layer breaks, you get an HTTP status of 400 or higher,

15
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:29,000
or a transport error writing inside the event stream. The request never produced a usable

16
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:34,000
answer. On the other side is the model output. That's the generation itself, and it rides on

17
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:41,000
a successful 200 response. The integration worked fine, but the content or the stop reason tells you

18
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:48,000
the answer is wrong, refused, or truncated. Same bad outcome, two completely different layers,

19
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:54,000
and the fix depends entirely on which one you're in. So how do you actually isolate the layer?

20
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:02,000
Read the trace from the outside in, one checkpoint at a time. Start with the HTTP status.

21
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:08,000
Anything in the 400 or 500 range is the integration layer, and you branch on the error type.

22
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:14,000
If the status was 200, don't stop there. Read the event stream, because an error can arrive

23
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:21,000
late, after the 200, as a streamed error event. And if you only check the status code, you'll miss

24
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:28,000
it entirely. If it's a clean 200 with content, now you inspect the stop reason and what the model

25
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:34,000
actually returned. And if there's a tool call that threw an exception or came back empty,

26
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:40,000
that points right back at your own harness. Stop at the first checkpoint that explains the

27
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:48,000
divergence. That checkpoint is your origin. Each layer announces itself in a different place,

28
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:55,000
so know where to look. An HTTP status code in the 400s or 500s means the request never produced

29
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:01,000
a usable answer, and you branch on the error type to pick a recovery. A streamed error event,

30
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:07,000
the late arrival you just met, still means integration failure, even though the status

31
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:13,000
said 200. That happens because streaming returns the status code up front, before generation

32
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:20,000
finishes, so a failure halfway through can only surface inside the stream. And the stop reason is

33
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:27,000
your model output tell. On a clean 200, it tells you whether the model finished naturally, ran out

34
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:34,000
of tokens, refused, or stopped for some other reason. Three places, three different meanings.

35
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:40,000
When you've confirmed you're on a clean 200, the stop reason tells you exactly what happened

36
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:47,000
in the generation, and three values are the model output tell. Max tokens means the response hit the

37
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:54,000
cap you set, truncated but valid. Model context window exceeded means the response filled the

38
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:00,000
model's total context window, not your requested cap. And refusal means Claude declined on policy

39
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:06,000
grounds, with the stop details carrying the category. You met the fix for each of these

40
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:12,000
in the previous lesson, so here the job is simpler. Recognize the signal, and you've isolated the

41
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:18,000
failure to the model output layer. Let's ground the integration layer in the usual suspects.

42
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:23,000
These bugs live in your code, even when the model's answer looks wrong on the surface.

43
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:29,000
A malformed request or a call to the wrong endpoint is common, and so is parsing the

44
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:35,000
response incorrectly and mangling it before your app ever sees it. Auth failures, billing problems,

45
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:42,000
and rate limit responses all sit in this layer, too. In the wrapper around the model. And a big

46
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:48,000
one for agents, a tool that threw an exception or returned an empty result. In every one of these,

47
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:54,000
the model may have behaved perfectly. The fix belongs in the harness, so resist the urge to go

48
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:01,000
rewrite your prompt. Now flip to the model output side. These originate in the generation itself,

49
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:07,000
even though every API call succeeded and every response parsed cleanly. Maybe the model chose

50
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:13,000
a wrong plan, despite having all the information it needed. Maybe it misread a tool result and fed

51
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:19,000
a bad value into the next step. Maybe it picked a bad tool argument, or the output came back as a

52
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:27,000
refusal or truncated and incomplete. Notice the pattern here. No HTTP error ever fired, so a

53
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:33,000
network retry does nothing. These are prompt and eval problems. You improve the instructions,

54
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:40,000
add examples, tighten the context, or run an evaluation to measure whether the change actually

55
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:47,000
helped. Retrying the identical request just reproduces the same answer. There's one more

56
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:53,000
origin question that trips up agent debugging, and it comes down to two possibilities when an eval

57
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:59,000
trial fails. The first, the agent genuinely made a mistake. The transcript shows flawed reasoning,

58
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:05,000
a wrong tool call, or a misread result. And the generation owns that failure, so you fix the

59
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:10,000
prompt or the model. The second, and this one's easy to miss, the agent was actually right, and

60
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:16,000
your grader or harness marked a valid answer as wrong. That's an integration bug in your eval,

61
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:21,000
not a model failure. The reliable way to tell them apart is to read the transcript,

62
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:25,000
checking both the process the agent followed and the final outcome in the environment.

63
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:32,000
Here's the bottom line. Origin isolation comes before the fix. The trace is your evidence,

64
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:39,000
not just the final output. HTTP codes and stream errors point to the integration layer.

65
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:44,000
A stop reason on a 200 points to the model output. Transcripts tell you whether the

66
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:49,000
agent or your grader failed. Isolate first, then edit only what diverged.

67
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:52,000
That wraps up this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

